# what mirl writes for x86-64: the selection table, the frame code, the
# moves, the loops and the short forms its record in
# mirl.target.register.isa joins to masc's set
#
# the table names the masc rows it emits, guards on the extensions masc admits
# for a row, and states which operations it leaves to a legalisation that
# comes before selection. its loops, in mirl.target.register.x86.loop, are the
# read-modify-writes no locked instruction does, which its rules select to.
# x86 has no configuration state an instruction runs under, so the record
# states none.

use mirl.target.register.x86.declared;
use mirl.target.register.x86.frame;
use mirl.target.register.x86.loop;
use mirl.target.register.x86.move;
use mirl.target.register.x86.rules;
use mirl.target.register.x86.short;

fwd rules.TABLE;
fwd declared.Declared;
fwd declared.DECLARED;
fwd frame.FRAME;
fwd move.MOVES;
fwd loop.LOOPS;
fwd short.SHORTS;
